o yeah, uh coming across one Hadith initially that's problematic isn't really going to take someone out of Sunnism. What usually happens is they come across the first and the second and then the third and then by the time they get to the fourth they think, \"Why am I even following Hadith in the first place? Why do I even believe in this stuff?\" Now, the reason why they don't leave Islam altogether or at least they don't claim to leave Islam altogether is because they don't view the Quran in the same way.", "If they find something problematic in the Quran, they will try to interpret it in a way to fit in with their values and uh it's sometimes easier to do that with the Quran because the Quran is not as rigid and as direct as the Hadith. So ultimately, what happens with Hadith rejecters is they hold onto the Quran alone and then they reject all Hadiths or that's what they claim. And the reason why I'm saying that is because with the vast majority of Hadith rejecters, they unknowingly accept Hadiths which affect the way they view the prophet, peace be upon him.", "Remember what I said earlier? When they come across a Hadith they don't like, they will say, \"There's no way that the prophet, peace be upon him, would do such a thing. There's no way he would say such a thing.\" And the reason why they say this is because they have a specific image of him in their minds. Now, this image would be based on his life. It would be based on the characteristics and the qualities of the prophet, peace be upon him. It would be about his struggles in Mecca. It could be about his struggles um in Medina.", "It could be about his mercy that he displayed upon um attaining victory against Quraish. It could be his qualities and personality when it comes to dealing with orphans or the poor. It could be a bunch of things. Whatever it is, the Hadith rejector has an image in their mind of the prophet peace be upon him, and due to that image, they will say things like, \"There's no way he would say that Hadith.\"",))
