#host("The Muslim Lantern", ("No, a Hadith is composed of.. OK, I’m going to explain to you, a Hadith is composed of two things: It's composed of the text of that Hadith: the incident that happened, the event, the speech that is told, etc. And then it's composed of something else, which we call a سَنَد (Sanad) which is a Chain of Narration of the people, individuals, that transmitted this information to us. Now, that Chain of Narration can be weak meaning, for example, one of the people, who narrates for another person could not have met him, He was born 200 years before him for example.", "So he couldn't have met him, or heard the information So then we would know this Chain of Narration is wrong. A person in the Chain of Narration is a liar, is a fabricator, he has an issue with his character.. We would reject this Narration Now, for every Hadith, we have a Chain of Narration, and we have the text itself. And for every Hadith, we will have it in numbers. Because every book of Hadith will have chapters and numbers of Ahadith. Now.. we have different books, we have Bukhari and Muslim, Which are the overwhelming majority of correct Narrations, meaning they're all Authentic Narrations Then we have other books, which can contain valid Narrations, and Narrations which are weak.", "For example, we have anNasa'i, we have atTirmidhi, we have ibnMaja.. We have all of these other books that have Narrations Now, you claim that there is a Narration. Can you please provide the source? Meaning, which book is this Narration mentioned in? and the number of the Hadith that you're quoting. Go Ahead",))
