#host("The Muslim Lantern", ("Let's talk about Hadith, what is a Hadith? A Hadith is that which was transmitted from the Prophetﷺ, from a statement So something that he said, or an action; a specific action that he did, or something that he approved of So something happened in the presence of the Prophetﷺ and he was OK with it, he didn't say to the people don't do or do not do Or the description of the Prophetﷺ, whether in his character, his manners, Or how he was created like his arms, his head, how he was created.. So anything of the statements, the sayings, the actions, the approvals, all of these things are what we call a Hadith That's the definition of what a Hadith is, and the Hadith is divided into 2 parts It is divided into what we call إسناد (Isnad) which is a Chain of Narration; The individuals who narrated this information, who gave us this information", "So we say Alex narrated from Mark, narrated from John, that he heard the Prophetﷺ say this.. So Alex and Mark and John, they are the Isnad; the Chain of Narration And that the Prophetﷺ said: \"1 plus 1 equals 2\" for example, hypothetically.. This statement, \"1 plus 1 equals 2\", the actual statement that the Prophetﷺ said, we consider that the متن (Matn) The Matn is the actual wording that the Prophetﷺ said, And the Isnad or the سَنَد (Sanad) is the Chain of Narration; the individuals who pass down this information And now all of these Ahadith, the statements and actions of the Prophetﷺ are generally divided Into 2 main categories: Authentic and Weak Now obviously there's a lot more different categories, but I don't want to go into deep, because I know people will get lost So we'll just stick with those 2 main categories..", "The Authentic Ahadith, what are these Authentic Ahadith? The Authentic Hadith is that which is transmitted from the Prophetﷺ by a person who is trustworthy A person who is known in his community to be a trustworthy individual, Who is a good individual and he's a practicing Muslim, and he’s a strong memoriser عدل ضابط The 1st condition is that he is a trustworthy individual, 2nd is that his memorisation and memory is very strong If it becomes weaker then he's no longer fitting that criteria So whoever he narrates from, has to also have these 2 conditions: Has to be strong in memory and has to be a good individual known in the community to be a good individual And the Chain has to be Connected.", "The Chain has to go all the way back to the Prophetﷺ, there cannot be any people missing in the Chain You cannot narrate from someone that was not living at your time, that was living 200 years before you Because you cannot meet him or you cannot take information from him. So it has to be possible for you to meet that individual Not only that, but also the Chain has to go all the way back to the Prophet If the information reaches a Companion of the Prophet only, but it does not reach the Prophetﷺ, and he doesn't say the Prophet said, Then that is a statement of a Companion, it is not a Hadith of the Prophetﷺ If it reaches a student of a Companion, then it is not a Narration of the Prophetﷺ, Rather it is a statement of a follower, of a Companion of the Prophetﷺ", "This is what Authentic Hadith is. That which is narrated by a trustworthy, just individual, well memoriser; a strong memory, Who narrates from someone like him, tracing all the way back to the Prophetﷺ And it cannot be irregular, they cannot have any defects, that Narration cannot have any defects Now defects and irregularity, these are like.. big things; that there's a whole Science written on that But I'll give a very basic example of شذوذ (Shudhudh: irregularity), which is for example where you have one person who told the story, And then there was six people sitting down, five of those people narrated the story in a way, And one individual narrated the same story, but he added extra information that the other five did not say, That would mean that this extra part that was added is either a mistake or irregularity It is something..", "the Narrator has made a mistake in attributing it to that Narrator when he did not narrate it So we know that; through tracing all of those different Chains of Narrations and we look at those people who narrated the Hadith, And we see if the majority are narrating one thing, and just one person who had an extra statement that the other people did not narrate –even though they've all heard the story from the same person– Then, we say the extra statement that was made is irregular and that statement can be rejected Also the idea of hidden defects; there are defects that can remove the reliability of a Hadith And there are defects that don't remove the reliability of the Hadith, the Hadith can be Authentic even though there's a hidden defect For instance, if we say that the Narrator says I heard from Ibn 'Umar, which is a Companion of the Prophet,", "And he actually heard it from Abu Hurayra, so both of those two different people are reliable, But the Narrator there's a hidden defect there, he made a mistake and he heard it from a wrong person He did not hear it from 'Abdullah Ibn 'Umar, he actually heard it from Abu Hurayra But he mixed the two individuals. So that's a defect, but a hidden defect Another hidden defect for example is someone narrates information; he attributes it to the Prophet, While actually, it was not from the Prophetﷺ but it was a story that was heard from the people of the Scriptures That, would be considered a hidden defect, and it will affect the Hadith Now the Hadith is no longer an Authentic Narration that you have to believe in Rather it's information taken from the people of the Scriptures As I said, I don't want to go into very strict details to show you how much those people who are rejecting Hadith", "Have no clue of how much work the people have put to authenticate these Narrations, And for us to come to this conclusion: these Narrations have to be historically reliable, So it's not just like we found the letter and the king was signing it, but we know who the king is, We have the full biography of the king, we know who wrote the letter, which handler of the king wrote the letter, We know his student who took the letter from him, and we know his son and his son and his son and his friend And whoever narrated that information until it reaches today, seeing it in front of our own eyes This is our criteria, not the criteria of the weak Western history that you accept, but our criteria is even further and much stricter for us to accept anything coming from the Prophetﷺ or not coming from the Prophetﷺ And how precise were those people actually in passing down the information",))
