#host("Farid Responds", ("The the first thing that I want you to be aware of, this is my first main argument is that when it comes to the hadith system, um there are two main conditions for determining the authenticity of a hadith. Those two conditions are the connectivity and the trustworthiness of the reports. Okay. Now what I want to get across to you guys is that this is not an exclusively Muslim thing. No. Since classical times you had Greek and Roman historians, they would all allude to well not all but when they would speak about um historioggraphy they would mention these conditions as conditions for authenticity.", "Okay. Emphasizing connectivity, trustworthiness long before Muslims did. I mean hundreds of years um before Muslims did. Okay. One of them is Marcelenus. He was the last great pagan historian. Yeah. He says, \"As far as I have been able to investigate the truth, we have narrated those matters which we were able to witness in our life or to know of by carefully examining those who were centrally engaged.\" Okay, he passed away slightly before the 4th century. Here, what he's saying is we need eyewitnesses. And you have similar statements like this from classical historians in the 16th and 17th centuries throughout Europe.", "What they used to do was they had these um historaphy uh manuals that teach you how to approach history. And these were penned again by um scholars in Europe and they had the same idea. One of these scholars uh is a French historian and theologian called Honore Daant Deant Marie and he passed away in the year 1713. He said as to well-known and not incredible events, one should accept without question the testimony of those writers under whose eyes they have happened or who have learned of them from honest, trustworthy contemporaries living in the country where the events occurred.", "A contemporary is a person living in the same age. As two events which are not public, but which contain nothing fabulous, one should regard them as probable when they occur in the country of the writer and highly probable when recounted by eyewitnesses or by contemporaries who do not seem to wish to deceive. And one more uh according to George uh Cornwall Lewis the author of inquiry to the credibility of early Roman history. He said unless these witnesses had personal and immediate perception of the facts which they report unless they saw and heard what they undertake to to relate as having happened.", "Their evidence is not entitled to credit as all original witnesses must be contemporary with the events which they attest. It is a necessary condition for the credibility of a witness that he be a contemporary though a contemporary is not necessarily a credible witness. Okay. So why am I saying this? It's because that's what Muslim Sunni scholars of hadith said in a gathering he would say and would stop him and he'd say what's wrong with you? May Allah destroy you. You report these narrations without using ropes. ropes meaning and it's not you got to mention who you're quoting when he was within every time would say don't say that you got to tell me who you're hearing from used to tell me don't you used to say don't say don't don't quote to me don't quote to me Ali because they don't care where their reports are getting from so you see when and when they start quoting the person they're narrating from, then it's accepted because now it's connected.", "When they don't quote their person, their their teacher kalas, it's something that is not accepted by early hadith scholars. So yeah, what's the point of me mentioning this? You may be asking yourself, well, the point is, my friends, that you need to be aware that scholars of hadith are aware that you need to be relying on eyewitnesses. It's not just scholars of hadith. It's the west uh European historians and ancient historians in Roman and Greek. Yeah. They say you need to know who you're hearing from. Um now the scholars of hadith not only paid attention to