you his work. He lists out the narrators. He quotes their hadiths and he shows you based on the narrations and based on what they're quoting whether they're reliable or not. It's not some sort of game. It's not some sort of guesswork. This is an actual study in actual science. And by the way, this is one this is one of the books. It's not there's tens of books like this. But I mean by a cool large book which is why I showed it as an example.", "Now the scholars they knew that just because some hadiths are weak it doesn't mean that all hadiths are weak. Just because some liars existed doesn't mean that every narrator is a liar. Just because some hadiths were disconnected it doesn't mean that every hadith was disconnected. If a narrator is established to be reliable if the chain is considered to be connected then the report is by default considered reliable. Again it's not only a Muslim position. It's a European position. It's a classical Greek and Roman position. So, of course, now I have to ask my interlocutor the question based on this background.", "And I I hope he answers this inshallah. Not not necessarily in the his introduction, but in his rebuttal. Um, if he feels like he wants to do it now, I don't mind. I mean, in his in his se section, inshallah. The question is this, and I hope he writes it down. Is the criteria provided for authenticity that I've mentioned as per the Muslim and non-Muslim historians? Is this criteria sufficient to determine the reliability of a report? If not, then what else is needed? I'm curious.",))
